id: pm-rl-zav1
title: "The proxy-to-held-out gap is the reward-hacking signal, measured per generation and trended across the chain"
description: "A self-improving loop rarely stops improving. It keeps improving on the objective being optimized while the objective the programme actually cares about goes flat or falls, and a programme that measures only the first cannot tell the two apart. Each generation therefore records both a proxy score, the quantity training optimized, and a held-out score on the pinned evaluation set. Recording two numbers is not enough to compare them, which was the gap in the first draft: the two scores must be attributed to the same standard and combined by a stated formula, or identical records produce different findings in different implementations. Both scores carry full provenance - the objective identifier, its version, and the evaluation context that produced them - so the proxy is identified as precisely as the held-out score rather than being a bare float. Each objective declares its direction, whether a higher value is better or a lower one is, and the gap is computed direction-aware so that a positive gap always means the proxy is ahead of held-out capability regardless of which way the underlying metric points. Scores from objectives with different units are normalized to their declared scales before subtraction, and objectives with no declared scale are refused rather than silently compared. The widening rule is a comparison of the gap across a window of consecutive promotions along one explicitly selected ancestry, never across a mixture of branches."
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-04T20:49:38.108Z"
